<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
	Deliberately NOT sandboxed, and this is a decision rather than an omission.

	The sandbox redirects HOME into ~/Library/Containers/<bundle id>/Data, which
	moves settings, presets, skins and delivery targets somewhere most people do
	not know exists. Open Audio Analyzer's configuration is designed to be edited by hand, mailed
	to somebody and kept in version control — see the Configuration section of
	README.md — and a container defeats all three. It also stops OAA_CONFIG_DIR
	from pointing anywhere outside itself, and the denial surfaces as "could not
	read <file>", which reads like a corrupt file rather than a permission.

	The cost is Mac App Store eligibility, which Open Audio Analyzer does not
	want: it is distributed as a pkg, a Windows installer, a tarball, an AppImage
	and a flatpak. Notarisation and the hardened runtime, which direct
	distribution does need, do not require the sandbox.

	The entitlements below are kept because they are correct declarations of what
	Open Audio Analyzer does, and because they are what the sandbox would need if it ever comes
	back. Do not re-add app-sandbox without moving the configuration story with
	it.
-->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The host picker's QR scanner. Required even though this app is
	     not sandboxed: a Developer ID build is signed with the hardened
	     runtime, which withholds the camera from a process that has not
	     asked for it here — and the refusal arrives as a camera that
	     never produces a frame rather than as an error. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
